"3cd7a5cc-e659-592c-a30b-217070eb9303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365400} :answer-field/id #uuid "4849f67e-73d4-59bc-b3d3-b0bcc5c71e3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365399}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96adb906-7cb9-5606-a5fd-4a0f9d2808bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-164440" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,6{x}^{2},\\,\\text{___}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=6{x}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,6{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $z.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec9f457-0a46-5786-943d-980831075831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365407} {:answer/id #uuid "0a987a72-f9a0-5a68-ba03-99fd4a6f8f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365408} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe46c52-aa0a-51fc-9267-b1401f5c53e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365409} {:answer/id #uuid "1510ed05-ccb6-5631-b10e-d3c6917da702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365410} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba30c13-8746-5d21-9cf7-d2bab32d7c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec9f457-0a46-5786-943d-980831075831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365407} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ab6088-4e94-502b-9bef-8ea9ffa9fd16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365406}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "903e7168-95b7-5bad-a739-c6d09fdecd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-164443" :question/problem "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{___},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x=3{y}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{y}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3{y}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043304f9-3a42-53ad-8538-3cea955fe040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389520} {:answer/id #uuid "8137e0c5-1440-5348-a3f6-f96860233599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389521} {:answer/id #uuid "6eea4516-42fb-5185-853e-429392d0e13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389522} {:answer/id #uuid "1720add8-c002-5c85-b431-35fa9200f452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389523} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4361d-c58f-5b04-80ef-1a4a55f43f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4361d-c58f-5b04-80ef-1a4a55f43f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389524} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ebc0e1f-5e97-5290-8156-5abbc7c3ef25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389519}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9e45fcb-4a47-5dc3-ae3f-636d48328fa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-164441" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,\\text{___},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=12{x}^{2}.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,12{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $12{x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c57d5c-a186-5182-951a-b3f85487caeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389527} {:answer/id #uuid "1f864458-d395-5710-9718-008110d8e93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389528} {:answer/id #uuid "0021c5a0-2342-5a2a-adac-8476ca592221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389529} {:answer/id #uuid "1602f014-a557-5551-9efb-7af44884ed34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389530} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cdf78f-30ac-56bf-91f8-1d994c3a2eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f864458-d395-5710-9718-008110d8e93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389528} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cb314a1-d9e4-51ef-9694-2ebcbc7cbfc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389526}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca6b56-70a9-5f2f-9046-3f8fb6cda9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164450" :question/problem "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{___},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $8{z}^{2}=x.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $8{z}^{2}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Parabolic Cylinder"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175973 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175823 :question/id #uuid "3c0c59e2-ceaa-54ab-bb56-3cb6daadb5f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8889" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y) = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, y, \\, \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\dfrac{z^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{16} = 1$ for $z \\geq 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x , z \\rangle = \\big \\langle 4\\tan{t}, \\, 2\\sec{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{sec}^{2}⁡t}{4}-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\sec{t}}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a36a5f81-d625-5eff-af81-12f7d91e02bb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048858 :topic/math-academy-id 873 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Horizontal Hyperbolas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048918 :topic/math-academy-id 2981 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Vertical Hyperbolas"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4bccfdf-8331-5efb-b37f-6bd4c45035fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173429} {:answer/id #uuid "f7003dda-c112-5f97-8bca-af47b02f7225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173430} {:answer/id #uuid "03e1293d-6cad-5a26-a81c-38d72cc2b3b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\csc t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173431} {:answer/id #uuid "44ea5614-9869-58f1-b629-974ac09bb962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173432} {:answer/id #uuid "490d325d-ffbe-5cd9-bf9c-2db184e68020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173433}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ab95b0a7-fefa-57b8-8081-55c2a3edc654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173428} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66f7a2b8-23c9-5110-8e24-f664a2447594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364662} {:answer/id #uuid "8b83da41-3c56-5bc4-85a9-468d168ca70b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364663} {:answer/id #uuid "775f11c6-37a0-5ac7-b461-a5d7115f3767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364664} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1b2546-f11f-5044-a149-48fa7c0b8940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364665} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff668a4-f940-589a-8790-3b71b79abc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc1b2546-f11f-5044-a149-48fa7c0b8940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364665} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a20c6d2-699b-56bb-8123-fa833c0c85c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364661}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c9aca83-9ce8-55b9-8ced-bafcb2d3c448" :question/math-academy-id "q-103998" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8976344d-d838-502e-b4be-975fe82dfee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174659} {:answer/id #uuid "ae331b4a-290d-5d0f-b08f-3fd871fb6ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\csc t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174660} {:answer/id #uuid "be3e7c50-063f-5d75-a9b8-b12f02b3bc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\csc t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174661} {:answer/id #uuid "c85348eb-7e75-5e90-81dd-82a2d5e78b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174662} {:answer/id #uuid "431a2779-b83a-5e9b-8edd-ecf661b3dc81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174663}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c58c139c-c01e-5255-91f7-33ebf0b26761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174658}] :question/id #uuid "4172de1d-e175-5c7f-99ca-9a107259ec3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164387" :question/problem "$f(x, t) = ⟨x, _____, 2\\sec t⟩, t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x, t)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3z^{2} - 4y^{2} = 12$ for $z \\ge 2$. What is the second component of $f$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d468ab70-401d-5a96-a5d9-10066682c534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365414} {:answer/id #uuid "29853ec6-e5eb-5f87-8737-fa642421e99f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365415} {:answer/id #uuid "562cf4d7-5216-576b-ab41-c7b6c344e636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365416} {:answer/id #uuid "5e10a9e5-20c8-571e-8fd6-6178d54ecf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365417} {:answer/id #uuid "06c3d2ac-5e98-5dde-9474-4bc695d82f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29853ec6-e5eb-5f87-8737-fa642421e99f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365415} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fc855bc-b950-554d-a402-a0a3bda2d624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365413}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "039615d1-3b91-5876-8c81-da27ba9dec92" :question/math-academy-id "q-164457" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,t)=⟨x,\\,\\text{_____},\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,t)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=49$ for $y\\ge 7.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=49$ for $y\\ge 7$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =49{sec}^{2}⁡t-49{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =49({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,t) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4207d3eb-db6b-589a-b486-b94a7c9598a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365421} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac48455-a05b-5a97-a250-069cc1619393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365422} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7faea4-f222-5378-b934-523cde58c436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365423} {:answer/id #uuid "cdadcbb0-995a-56d8-80a6-25c096de8d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365424} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd196ef-bf7a-5f13-a3cc-fc6226a49d7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4207d3eb-db6b-589a-b486-b94a7c9598a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365421} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb20991-ac0a-5616-8a70-0419a9707880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365420}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c46d3a87-a39a-50a3-a78d-09833afff4e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-164519" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,y,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b007fc6-a9cf-594b-9130-ef77ac13f04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389513} {:answer/id #uuid "da47b9c2-8db5-5641-8844-3d6f057e449c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389514} {:answer/id #uuid "32a02b40-341b-55c1-bb30-36fc4d4ff206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389515} {:answer/id #uuid "8d89ca9e-8cf1-5b9c-a38d-3f29dfc620a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389516} {:answer/id #uuid "7881fdc3-f1e6-5655-83ea-d156cd6e11c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d89ca9e-8cf1-5b9c-a38d-3f29dfc620a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389516} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92fac85-7a9a-5915-90d4-a283e5eef049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38414465-84de-53ed-9f2d-540277ea9f01" :question/math-academy-id "q-164439" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18\\,⇒\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{sec}^{2}⁡t}{9}-\\frac{2{tan}^{2}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "980f0e6e-b727-5884-9358-8f4cc871929f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389534} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd57ebb-6b0c-59bf-a69b-ea61588fa5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389535} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5a90d4-f70c-5dbb-86f2-5da3595c4717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389536} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4ce062-cc41-523a-b403-73151b819608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389537} {:answer/id #uuid "3fca4567-695f-5afb-931c-d416f8324d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5a90d4-f70c-5dbb-86f2-5da3595c4717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389536} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb03e48f-e4f7-56b0-b0ed-a15eec593f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389533}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59a480e6-23f1-5a37-b498-2c012faa9753" :question/math-academy-id "q-164521" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}-{x}^{2}=4$ for $z\\ge 2.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{x}^{2}=4$ for $z\\ge 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡t-4{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Hyperbolic Cylinder"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179725 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179587 :question/id #uuid "17ec17a0-11d7-57c1-9e7f-6893ab9e51e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9487" :question/problem "Find the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t[(-\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)-(j-3k)] \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t(-i+\\,j+k) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+(t+1)\\,j+(t-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)k \\\\  & =-i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14ab317b-fd33-5d6e-9d54-87e749b78283" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd85cf1-6e5c-526a-9bea-91297855754b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177562} {:answer/id #uuid "9a07dfd3-fada-503a-8d7d-7c5d05270d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177563} {:answer/id #uuid "9f33e2da-0a3f-5c94-ad41-0fb76655fa97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 - t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177564} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1e4d45-b42c-5544-b771-2c4dec44fb67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 + 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177565} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1d15f6-2c98-5679-a3a6-3f9a91e6f361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177566}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b2227ecd-9c94-5c91-8bd5-29870aa8a181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177561}] :question/id #uuid "471bb8a9-9025-5051-9cad-6b9d143c1f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-123128" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y, z) = x + y + z$, traversed along the line segment from $(1, 0, 1)$ to $(2, 3, 0)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a68544-3dee-56d2-b4c7-eeed22d1e2cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179213} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9036a3-920f-5615-bfeb-f807ba857652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179214} {:answer/id #uuid "d94aea65-e2a6-5fe5-abd3-22496a932674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179215} {:answer/id #uuid "15c8a75a-7643-52b0-b284-d4374f9fef41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179216} {:answer/id #uuid "173fb852-8a5c-5d4b-acb1-8126fdbffabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179217}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6f151015-d188-52ae-99fa-d6c0a2bc7f89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179212}] :question/id #uuid "bd6e5c6e-c45a-513e-b007-721402a77203" :question/math-academy-id "q-76200" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\sin (\\frac{πy}{4})ds$ along the line segment from $(1, 0, 1)$ to $(2, 2, 3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7255fb-f802-5c49-9cfe-5adaba813eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257598} {:answer/id #uuid "68a17d3d-a884-5f62-9df5-f64e05ce9233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257710} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b81ee3-92f3-5eba-89d3-57cdd78eceac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258623} {:answer/id #uuid "e1093dce-dfaa-5005-8422-51b5c1f47ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258741} {:answer/id #uuid "f4307381-173f-58dd-8909-ee98e9452ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2t+1}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1093dce-dfaa-5005-8422-51b5c1f47ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258741} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f56fccf-0333-5fb0-960a-7e7e292ac899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259086}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bda2dabe-428d-5884-9572-9eaa49f96d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123431" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ traversed along the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-4i+j+5\\,k$ and $b=-2\\,i+3\\,j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-4i+j+5\\,k)+t[(-2\\,i+3\\,j+3\\,k)-(-4i+j+5\\,k)] \\\\  & =(-4i+j+5\\,k)+t(2i+2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j+(5-2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-4,y=2t+1,$ and $z=5-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-2t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(2t+1)+(5-2t)}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{t-2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d242074-73a7-5026-be5b-fcc25f37d64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}({e}^{3}+2)}{{e}^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257185} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8b5c20-0f40-57fe-b657-0025210be1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{2}-1)}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257755} {:answer/id #uuid "c38917a5-66c0-5027-9c6a-4c2921700ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258496} {:answer/id #uuid "d03cd34c-c515-5229-bf1b-a2f918c9c671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{{e}^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258587} {:answer/id #uuid "e913cf48-832b-5dd0-a3e6-66ca570be19c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{{e}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c38917a5-66c0-5027-9c6a-4c2921700ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258496} :answer-field/id #uuid "083a3adb-7461-5e3e-8a79-8a563a559eed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259050}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a763ea78-00d8-589b-9ab8-583f7360f0c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123434" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k$ and $b=4\\,i-\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)+t[(4\\,i-\\,j+5\\,k)-(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)+t(2\\,i+j+k) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(t+4)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+2,$ $y=t-2,$ and $z=t+4$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t+2){e}^{(t-2)(t+4)} \\\\  & =(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+2t-8$, $\\text{d}u=(2t+2)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\int }_{-8}^{-5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{-8}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}({e}^{-5}-{e}^{-8}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{8}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d469bce-94af-5634-ab82-5aa6af354e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342616} {:answer/id #uuid "8209f70f-983a-5ae7-86d1-4e93d816e7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} {:answer/id #uuid "85c4d835-de5f-5323-ac7e-857e358d872b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343386} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d73c23-a898-521d-b2b3-a61c11d3d91a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343697} {:answer/id #uuid "baa7a4d0-7ea9-5d62-9f82-07ada8a4ece3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8209f70f-983a-5ae7-86d1-4e93d816e7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b45262a-d4fd-5260-bbcc-f503f4fcadd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ad0c20-f0d9-5a56-ac84-89e9373c74a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76569" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from$(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=2\\,i+j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[(2\\,i+j+3\\,k)-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(i-j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(2-t)\\,j+3t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t,$ $y=2-t,$ and $z=3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)k \\\\  & =i-j+3\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)(2-t)^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}+4t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}(\\frac{1}{4}-1+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9bcbed-0436-52a7-8a8d-e2de93c0b880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} {:answer/id #uuid "368a2103-e780-5582-ad6f-3a21aa3a8980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342688} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbd128-6385-59fc-8240-9d369f5dd5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343182} {:answer/id #uuid "b163fa1b-99f8-590b-a1f2-94f8b252cb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343793} {:answer/id #uuid "bc47b99b-71b1-565f-a23d-6d3b20733bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e9bcbed-0436-52a7-8a8d-e2de93c0b880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed33cfa-1f9a-54f9-9d11-cd56d8af78bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344940}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07d5db28-c47d-5f6c-be75-b0e883a1921b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76577" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j+k$ and $b=i+2\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t[(i+2\\,j+5\\,k)-(-i+2\\,j+k)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,k) \\\\  & =(-1+2t)\\,i+2\\,j+(1+4t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-1+2t,$ $y=2,$ and $z=1+4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+4t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+0+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}((-1+2t)+(1+4t))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2e9acc-3c64-51a7-9ab5-d350f9449ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387383} {:answer/id #uuid "89ac3f7c-fb26-5086-aea8-2db3445e4653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387384} {:answer/id #uuid "ef97d2f5-fde9-5e22-82b7-c60bd3fea467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387385} {:answer/id #uuid "ec794748-a3c8-58b6-8929-a9cf739d2848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{5t-3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387386} {:answer/id #uuid "26182461-9d9e-569f-b647-6e1cb1f93a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26182461-9d9e-569f-b647-6e1cb1f93a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387387} :answer-field/id #uuid "36154aca-9593-55ae-8c89-27fef1f72807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387382}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "963025de-8b37-535e-a08b-0b16983c48ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-123387" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z},$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3i-2\\,j+3k$ and $b=5\\,i-j+7\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t[(5\\,i-j+7\\,k)-(3i-2\\,j+3k)] \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t(2i+\\,j+4k) \\\\  & =(2t+3)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(4t+3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+3,y=t-2,$ and $z=4t+3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+3)k \\\\  & =2i+j+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2(2t+3)+(t-2)-(4t+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Along Segments in Three Dimensions"}]}
